\begin_layout Section
Complaxity Measure with the Tree Edit Distance
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this part we discuss possibilities to make measurable explanations.
 As a result, the Tree Edit Distance (TED) is introduced as a measure of
 familiarity that can be used in GP.
 Furthermore, limitations and possible improvements are discussed.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Vertrautheitsmaß
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The explainability distance to the potential candidates are resulted in
 the following considerations for a complexity measurement: 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "xaiMeasurementRequirements"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Fulfilling the prerequisites for a distance measure 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $d(x,y)\ge0$
\end_inset

 (non-negativity)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $d(x,y)=0\Leftrightarrow x=y$
\end_inset

 (identity of indiscernibles) 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $d(x,y)=d(y,x)$
\end_inset

 (symmetry) 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $d(x,y)\le d(x,z)+d(z,y)$
\end_inset

 (triangle inequality) 
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Measure small differences
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Univariate or low-dimansional multivariate formula which is based on a few
 paradigms
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Not fundamentally limiting the pareto GP
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Weaknesses and conceivable improvements
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Weighted TED
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

The weighting of the nodes is not a trivial task with the TED.
 Therefore the weighted mapping of a tree transformation must be calculated,
 which is not unique and also leaves open questions about how weightings
 should affect the respective changes.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The absolute complexity of the origin is chosen as initial complexity, if
 nodes are removed or changed a part of the weighting is subtracted
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
A separate parsimony measurement (possibly as a new dimension of the Pareto
 front)
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Explicitly secure the core structure (fixed nodes).
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

As early experiments should show, the TED can lose its protective effect
 if the changes overcome the tree size.
 This is especially true for Origin trees with few nodes or strongly abstracting
 structures (e.g.
 frequent use of if statements).
 In extreme cases, the TED often differs only slightly from node-counting
 distance dimensions.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

When the implicit protection of logical core structures by the TED is not
 sufficient, then logic relevant structures with a very high weight can
 be made robust against changes.
 As extreme form also fixed nodes, which shall be untouched by changes,
 can be defined.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Fixed-nodes"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Preferred Counting of operators
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

Die Struktur eines Baumes wird im wesentlichen durch die Anordnungsstruktur
 seiner Operatoren bestimmt.
 Die Blattknoten, vor allem die Konstanten, fallen hier mit einem relativ
 hohen Anteil ins Gewicht der TED, obwohl die Änderungen oft nur geringfügige
 Anpassungen ohne substantielle Änderung sind.
 Konstanten sollten deshalb weitaus weniger ins Gewicht fallen.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Verwendung unvollständiger Strukturen als origin (z.
 B.
 fehlende Terminal-Knoten)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Verwendung mehrerer bekannter origins in einem Lauf, wobei beispielsweise
 die kleinste oder mittlere TED verwendet wird.
 So könnten auch mehrere verständliche Ansätze auf ihre Erweiterbarkeit
 direkt verglichen werden oder mehrere Teillösungen separat eingebracht
 werden.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The occurrence of variables could be weighted separately with a relatively
 high value to force a reduced use of them.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Fixed Nodes
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Fixed-nodes"

\end_inset


